.TH CYPACHE\-CONFIG 5 2026-10-30 Cypache "Cypache Manual"
.ds AD l
.nh
.SH NAME
cypache\-config \- the Python script that configures Cypache
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import cypache

site = cypache.Site("example.com", "www.example.com", root="/srv/example/public",
                    apps="/srv/example/controllers", views="/srv/example/views",
                    tls="/etc/cypache/tls/example.com")
cypache.serve([site], http=80, https=443, acme="/var/www/acme",
              accessLog="/var/log/cypache/access.log")
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Cypache's configuration is a Python script.
It builds a
.B Site
for each virtual host and calls
.BR serve() ,
which serves them until the server stops.
The Debian package runs
.I /etc/cypache/config.py
with
.BR "/opt/cypache/venv/bin/python \-I" ;
.B \-I
keeps Python from importing modules from the script's directory or the working directory, and from reading
.B PYTHON*
variables.
.PP
The script runs with the server's privileges, at every start, and with
.B reload
once more for each new generation of the code (see RELOAD).
It must not be writable by the server's user.
.PP
Arguments that make no sense, such as a timeout that isn't a positive number, an unknown host name or a directory that doesn't exist, raise
.B ValueError
before anything is served.
.SH SITES
.EX
cypache.Site(*hosts, routes=None, root=None, index=("index.html",),
             precompressed=False, cacheControl=None, apps=None, views=None,
             tls=None, hsts=31536000, default=False)
.EE
.TP
.I hosts
Bare names or addresses
.RB ( \(dqexample.com\(dq ,
.BR \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq ,
.BR \(dq[::1]\(dq ),
matched case\-insensitively against the request's Host without its port or a trailing dot.
IPv6 addresses match in any spelling.
.TP
.B default
A request for a host no Site lists goes to the Site with
.BR default=True ,
or gets
.B 421 Misdirected Request
if there is none.
There is no implicit default, so a site never answers for domains pointed at it by others (DNS rebinding).
.PP
A request is served by the first of these that takes it:
.TP
.B routes
A dict of exact, normalized paths to
.BR handler(request) ;
see
.BR cypache\-apps (7).
.TP
.B root
A directory of static files, for GET and HEAD (see STATIC FILES).
A file that isn't there, or a directory requested without its trailing slash, is left to the apps when there are any, like nginx's
.BR try_files .
.TP
.BR apps ", " views
Directories of Python modules, routed by file; see
.BR cypache\-apps (7).
.PP
And:
.TP
.B index
The files tried, in order, for a static path ending in
.BR / .
.TP
.B precompressed
Serve
.I style.css.br
or
.I .gz
copies next to the files to clients that accept them.
Off by default, since looking for them costs two opens per request.
.TP
.B cacheControl
A
.B Cache\-Control
value for static responses, such as
.BR \(dqpublic,\ max\-age=3600\(dq .
Without it, caches guess freshness from the file's age.
.TP
.B tls
A directory with
.I fullchain.pem
and
.I privkey.pem
(certbot's layout), or a
.RI ( certificate ", " key )
pair of files.
Keys must be unencrypted.
See HTTPS.
.TP
.B hsts
The
.B Strict\-Transport\-Security
max\-age every HTTPS response of the Site carries, a year by default;
.B 0
turns it off.
Turn it off before dropping HTTPS for a domain, then wait out the old max\-age.
.B includeSubDomains
and
.B preload
aren't sent, since they commit other names too.
.PP
No Site's static root, nor the
.B acme
directory, may overlap any Site's apps or views:
this is checked at startup, and again every second for a root that is a symlink, which then gets
.B 503
if it was switched onto code.
.SH OPTIONS
.EX
cypache.serve(sites, http=8080, https=None, host=None, acme=None, ...)
.EE
.TP
.BR http =8080
Port or list of ports for plain HTTP;
.B None
for none.
.TP
.BR https =None
Port or list of ports for HTTPS; needed when a Site has
.BR tls .
.TP
.BR host =None
Address or addresses to listen on;
.B None
is all interfaces.
.TP
.BR acme =None
A certbot webroot, served for
.I /.well\-known/acme\-challenge/
over plain HTTP for every Site.
.TP
.BR workers =0
Worker processes:
.B 0
is one per CPU the server may run on (its affinity, as
.BR taskset (1)
or a container sets it),
.B 1
serves in the calling process.
.TP
.BR threads =16
Handler threads per worker.
.TP
.BR handlerTimeout =120
Seconds before a running handler is reported as stuck;
.B None
turns that off.
.TP
.BR headerTimeout =15
Seconds to receive a complete request head, from the connection or the previous request.
Idle keep\-alive connections close after it.
.TP
.BR bodyTimeout =60
Seconds a request body may fall behind
.BR minBodyRate :
to start, or stalled.
.TP
.BR minBodyRate =8192
Bytes per second a request body must arrive at, and a response be taken at, on average (see TIMEOUTS).
.TP
.BR idleTimeout =60
Seconds without any I/O progress, such as a client that stops reading.
.TP
.BR handshakeTimeout =10
Seconds to finish the TLS handshake.
.TP
.BR maxConnections =10000
Connections per worker.
.B serve()
raises the soft open\-file limit to 4 \(mu this + 64, and refuses to start if the hard limit is lower
.RB ( LimitNOFILE=
in systemd).
.TP
.BR shutdownTimeout =10
Seconds to finish open requests after SIGTERM or SIGINT.
.TP
.BR maxHeaderBytes =16384
Bytes of request line and header fields
.RB ( 431 ,
or
.B 414
for the request line).
.TP
.BR maxHeaders =100
Header fields
.RB ( 431 ).
.TP
.BR maxBodyBytes =1048576
Bytes of a request body
.RB ( 413 ).
Sites that take uploads raise it.
.TP
.BR maxBodyMemory =67108864
Request body bytes received and held in memory at once, per worker
.RB ( 503 ).
The memory holding them can reach about twice that while bodies grow.
.TP
.BR spoolBytes =1048576
Larger bodies are written to a spool file while they arrive.
.TP
.BR maxSpoolBytes =1073741824
Spooled body bytes at once, per worker
.RB ( 503 ).
.TP
.BR spoolDir =/var/tmp
Where spool files are made: unnamed temporary files
.RB ( O_TMPFILE ),
gone once closed.
It's opened once at startup, must belong to root or the server's user, and its filesystem must support
.BR O_TMPFILE .
.TP
.BR accessLog =None
The access log's file (see ACCESS LOG);
.B None
for none.
.TP
.BR http2 =True
HTTP/2 over HTTPS, for clients that offer it.
.TP
.BR serverName =\(dqCypache\(dq
The
.B Server
header's value;
.B \(dq\(dq
omits it.
.TP
.BR reload =True
Serve the new code a moment after a
.B .py
changes, once it loads (see RELOAD).
.TP
.BR watch =()
Folders whose subfolders appearing or going reload too, with
.BR reload .
.SH TIMEOUTS
Timeouts can't be turned off.
.IP \(bu 2
A request body must keep arriving at
.B minBodyRate
on average: each byte buys 1/\fBminBodyRate\fR seconds, banked up to
.B bodyTimeout
ahead.
A body arriving that fast never times out, whatever its size; one that stalls gets
.B 408
within
.BR bodyTimeout .
.IP \(bu 2
A client still receiving an earlier response must take it at
.B minBodyRate
on average, each byte buying 1/\fBminBodyRate\fR seconds, banked up to
.B headerTimeout
ahead.
One that falls behind is disconnected and the rest of its response dropped, so a client reading slowly can't hold a connection for hours.
HTTP/2 connections are held to the same rate for all their responses together.
.IP \(bu 2
Sites with clients on very slow links lower
.BR minBodyRate .
The rate is also what holding a budget costs: a client that sends most of a body at once and the rest at the rate keeps that much of
.B maxBodyMemory
or
.B maxSpoolBytes
for up to
.BR maxBodyBytes / minBodyRate
seconds.
A site taking large uploads raises
.BR minBodyRate ,
or the budgets, to match.
.IP \(bu 2
fastepoll's
.B idleTimeout
drops a connection without any I/O for that long, also one whose handler runs.
.SH HTTPS
.IP \(bu 2
Each Site's certificate is sent to the names it serves (SNI).
Clients without SNI, or with a name no Site has, get the certificate of the default Site, or of the first Site with
.B tls
if that one has none.
.IP \(bu 2
A request whose Host belongs to another Site than the certificate the client checked gets
.BR 421 :
one certificate can't be used to reach another site on the same connection (domain fronting).
.IP \(bu 2
A Site with
.B tls
answers plain HTTP with
.B 308
to the same URL over HTTPS, on the first
.B https
port.
A request with a body is redirected without reading it, so the body isn't sent in the clear.
Sites without
.B tls
are served over plain HTTP.
.IP \(bu 2
Without
.BR acme ,
challenge requests for Sites with
.B tls
are redirected to HTTPS like everything else, which Let's Encrypt follows.
.IP \(bu 2
TLS is fastepoll's: TLS 1.2 and 1.3 with forward\-secret AEAD ciphers only, post\-quantum hybrid key exchange where OpenSSL has it, and no renegotiation, 0\-RTT or compression.
With the kernel's
.B tls
module, on Linux 6.14 and later, files are sent with zero\-copy
.BR sendfile ()
through kernel TLS; older kernels can't change TLS 1.3 keys on a live connection, so there TLS is in userspace.
.IP \(bu 2
SIGHUP loads the certificates anew, for new connections.
If they don't load, the previous ones stay in use and the error is printed.
.SH HTTP/2
HTTPS speaks HTTP/2 to clients that offer it (ALPN
.BR h2 ),
and HTTP/1.1 to the rest.
There is no HTTP/2 over plain HTTP and no server push.
The framing is nghttp2's, with its defenses against known floods.
.IP \(bu 2
Each stream's request goes to the HTTP/1 parser as an HTTP/1.1 message with version
.BR HTTP/2.0 ,
so every rule here applies to it.
.B :authority
is the Host, and a
.B host
field that differs from it gets
.BR 400 .
.IP \(bu 2
A field nghttp2 would silently drop (control characters, surrounding whitespace) resets the stream instead.
A client that provokes more than 1000 resets at once, then 33 a second, is disconnected (MadeYouReset).
.IP \(bu 2
At most 32 streams per connection, counting reset streams whose handlers still run (Rapid Reset).
A stream may send 1 MiB of body ahead, a connection 16 MiB, counted against the body budgets.
.IP \(bu 2
A connection without streams gets GOAWAY after
.BR headerTimeout .
A connection whose responses wait for the client holds up to 32 of them, and per worker such responses may hold a quarter of the open\-file limit; a stream over that is refused.
.SH STATIC FILES
.IP \(bu 2
Every file is opened with
.BR openat2 (2)
and
.BR RESOLVE_BENEATH ", " RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS " and " RESOLVE_NO_XDEV
relative to the root: the kernel refuses anything outside it, symlinks inside it aren't followed, and mount points aren't crossed.
The root itself may be a symlink, and is reopened every second, so
.B "ln \-s release2 next && mv \-T next current"
switches to a new release atomically.
Replace single files by renaming a new one over them.
.IP \(bu 2
Never served, with
.BR 404 :
hidden files (a path segment starting with
.BR . ,
except a leading
.IR /.well\-known/ );
Python and Cython source, bytecode and notebooks, also compressed or as editor and backup copies;
backup files
.RB ( \(ti ", " .bak ", " .old ", " .orig ", " .save ", " .swp );
symlinks; and anything but regular files and directories.
Unreadable files get
.BR 403 .
.IP \(bu 2
The root must be on ext2/3/4, btrfs, XFS, tmpfs, F2FS, overlayfs, ZFS or bcachefs, and not in a casefold directory;
anything else, where other spellings of a name could get past these rules, is refused at startup.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory requested without its trailing slash gets
.B 308
to add it.
There are no directory listings.
Methods other than GET and HEAD get
.BR 405 .
.IP \(bu 2
Content\-Type comes from a fixed table of extensions, with
.B charset=utf\-8
for text; unknown ones are
.BR application/octet\-stream .
Every response carries
.BR "X\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B ETag
is made of the size, the nanosecond mtime and the inode.
Conditional requests are evaluated in RFC 9110 order, and
.B If\-Range
only accepts an entity tag.
One byte range per request is served
.RB ( 206 ", " 416 );
a request for several gets the whole file.
.IP \(bu 2
Files up to 16 KiB go out with the head in one write, larger ones with zero\-copy
.BR sendfile (2).
A connection gets its next pipelined request answered only once the previous response is out, so a client that doesn't read can hold at most one open file.
.SH RELOAD
With
.BR reload=True ,
the process that runs the configuration binds the ports and becomes the launcher, which keeps the listening sockets and forks a generation to run the configuration again and serve them.
So the configuration runs twice at startup, and must be a file, a directory or zip file with a
.IR __main__.py ,
or a module run with
.BR \-m .
.IP \(bu 2
The launcher looks twice a second at every
.B .py
in the apps and views directories, and at the files of the other modules the generation loaded, such as the configuration and a site's own package.
Folders named in
.B watch
reload when a subfolder appears or goes.
.IP \(bu 2
Once the changes settle, it starts the next generation, which loads all the code while the previous one serves.
If it loads, it serves the launcher's sockets at once, and the previous one finishes its requests as on SIGTERM.
The ports never go free.
Code that doesn't load, or a configuration that now listens elsewhere (which takes a restart), is reported on stderr, and the previous code keeps serving.
.IP \(bu 2
With reload, a
.B .py
runs a moment after it appears, so the code directories must be writable by root only.
The server's processes write no
.IR __pycache__ .
.IP \(bu 2
.B reload=False
imports once, in the calling process: code changes then need a restart.
.SH ACCESS LOG
.EX
203.0.113.7 \- \- [28/Oct/2026:12:03:16 +0100] "GET /news HTTP/1.1" 200 5120 "https://example.com/" "Mozilla/5.0 ..." "example.com" 0.006
.EE
.PP
One line per response, in the combined format that log analyzers and
.BR fail2ban (1)
read: the client's address, the local time, the request line as received, the status, the body bytes sent (0 for HEAD and 304),
.B Referer
and
.BR User\-Agent ;
then the Host the request was routed by
.RB ( \-
when it failed before its head was parsed), and the seconds from the complete request head to the response being handed on.
.IP \(bu 2
As in nginx,
.BR \(dq ,
.B \e
and bytes outside printable ASCII appear as
.BI \ex HH\c
, so a client can't forge a line.
An empty field is
.BR \- .
.IP \(bu 2
A request whose connection closed before its handler was done is logged as
.B 499
(nginx's code): the client reset it (or over HTTPS, which has no half\-close, closed its side), or a timeout or shutdown cut it.
A connection that closes while idle, or before its request fully arrived, gets no line.
.IP \(bu 2
The file must be a regular file.
Each worker checks once a second whether it was renamed or removed, and opens it anew, so it can be rotated without signals.
When writing fails, the server goes on serving, prints the error once, and later how many lines were lost.
.SH REQUESTS
The HTTP/1.1 parser refuses with
.B 400
(or the status given):
.IP \(bu 2
anything but CRLF line endings, obs\-fold, whitespace before a colon, control characters in a field;
.IP \(bu 2
both
.B Content\-Length
and
.BR Transfer\-Encoding ,
a repeated
.BR Content\-Length ,
a length that isn't plain digits, any transfer coding but
.BR chunked ,
chunked framing in HTTP/1.0;
.IP \(bu 2
a missing or repeated
.B Host
in HTTP/1.1, or a malformed one;
.IP \(bu 2
targets that aren't origin\-form, or
.B *
for OPTIONS;
.B #
in a target;
.IP \(bu 2
paths with bad escapes, NUL or control characters,
.BR \e ,
an escaped
.B /
.RB ( %2F ),
invalid UTF\-8, or
.B ..\&
above the root;
.IP \(bu 2
TRACE and CONNECT
.RB ( 405 ),
unknown methods
.RB ( 501 ),
HTTP versions other than 1.x
.RB ( 505 ).
.PP
A path spelled in a way a proxy's rules could read differently, with dot or empty segments, needless escapes or lowercase hex, gets
.B 308
to its clean form, so a proxy in front applies its rules to exactly the path that is served.
Responses the server makes itself, errors and redirects, carry
.BR "Cache\-Control: no\-store" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Two sites over HTTPS, one taking uploads of up to 100 MiB:
.PP
.EX
import cypache

TLS = "/etc/cypache/tls"
shop = cypache.Site("shop.example", "www.shop.example", root="/srv/shop/public",
                    apps="/srv/shop/controllers", views="/srv/shop/views",
                    tls=f"{TLS}/shop.example")
blog = cypache.Site("blog.example", root="/srv/blog/public",
                    tls=f"{TLS}/blog.example", default=True)
cypache.serve([shop, blog], http=80, https=443, acme="/var/www/acme",
              accessLog="/var/log/cypache/access.log",
              maxBodyBytes=100 << 20, minBodyRate=32768)
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cypache (8),
.BR cypache\-apps (7),
.BR certbot (1),
.BR openat2 (2)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/cypache/README.md
has the complete documentation.
